**Read:** [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), chapter 7 in full; [Introduction to Historical Geology — Estrada and contributing authors](../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD), metamorphism, mountain building, and the corresponding historical examples; [Introduction to Geology — Perron and Jagoutz, MIT 12.001](../sources/MIT-Introduction-to-Geology.lingua#r_423FFVY46CGEN2F2263F95KX6N), the metamorphic identification and structure material.

Metamorphism connects changes in mineral assemblage, chemistry, texture, and fabric with pressure, temperature, fluids, deformation, time, and protolith. Distinguish an observed assemblage from an inferred equilibrium condition or complete path. A rock may preserve reactions and structures from more than one event.

Foliation, lineation, porphyroblasts, reaction textures, and relations with intrusions or faults can constrain a sequence. Grade and facies are useful frameworks whose compositional and equilibrium assumptions matter. An index mineral's occurrence alone cannot specify one exact pressure, temperature, or age for every protolith.

Fluids and open-system transfer can alter composition, while kinetic limits and retrogression can change or preserve evidence. A mapped metamorphic pattern needs a connection between specimens, analytical methods, structural context, and time information. Several paths may be consistent with an incomplete record.

**Work:** reconstruct an identified textbook metamorphic case from description through competing pressure/temperature/deformation histories. Distinguish a protolith inference from a measured composition and from a time constraint. Propose observations and analyses needed to test a claimed path, preserving assumptions and uncertainty.

Read the entire metamorphic chapter and historical examples, then connect them with the later structural and geochronological sequence. The specialist mineralogy/petrology and geochemistry scopes will deepen thermobarometry, reaction models, and analytical research; general geology should already support a coherent, evidence-based interpretation.

**Continue:** [Order events from relationships before assigning numerical ages](../time/Relative-ordering-and-geological-events.lingua#r_0KXVTSWTTJQ9GASZX3Y90Z5YNN).

